  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Sun May 24 2026\n\nThe forecast starting with the middle of the upcoming week looks \nactive. An upper-level low deepening over the Pacific Northwest will \ndig southward into California on Tuesday. A smaller, negatively-\ntilted trough will be pushed out ahead of the main upper low into \nwest Texas by Wednesday night. Ahead of this feature, the airmass \nwill be moist with dewpoints in the 60s and PWATs of 1.25-1.5 \ninches. Models indicate storms will develop along the dryline and \nthe higher terrain across west Texas and the Permian Basin in the \nafternoon. Storms will then move east-northeastward through west-\ncentral Texas with the upper-level flow through Tuesday night.  In \nthe afternoon, 0-6km shear values of 30-40KT are expected so some \nstorms could become strong to severe with large hail, especially \nacross the Edwards Plateau and western Concho Valley. Overnight, \nthis will likely transition into a widespread heavy rain event as \nstorms congeal into a line and move slowly across the region through \nWednesday morning. There is a medium (40-60%) chance of rainfall \nexceeding one inch from Tuesday morning through Thursday morning, \nand this could lead to flash flooding concerns. For now, WPC has \nposted a Slight Risk of Excessive Rainfall for Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight and a Flood Watch may need to be considered.\n\nStorm chances look much lower for Thursday and Friday as upper-level \nridging will build in across west central Texas. Temperatures could \npush up to around 90 both days due to the lack of rain and cloud \ncover.\n\nThe pattern looks to become more active again this weekend as models \nshow another upper-level shortwave trough moving out of northwest \nMexico and into west Texas.  For now, this would place the region \nunder southwest flow aloft, and lead to additional rounds of \nthunderstorms on Saturday and Sunday.  There is still some \ninconsistency in how models are handling the upper-level flow so for \nnow timing and storm type are uncertain until models come into \nbetter agreement.",
